//     `No Collision`  Transform + TilemapRenderer + Tilemap. NO COLLIDER AT
//                     ALL, so a raycast can never hit it. (The old text implied
//                     a ray could hit it and "tell you that you are standing on
//                     scenery" — corrected: it is paint.)
//     `Collision`     TilemapCollider2D + CompositeCollider2D + Rigidbody2D
//                     (m_BodyType: 2 = STATIC, m_GravityScale: 1). Tag `Ground`,
//                     layer 0 (Default). This is the floor.
//     `Cave Exit`     the SAME three components and the SAME Static body, but
//                     m_TagString: Untagged, layer 0 (Default). It carries
//                     SceneSwitch.cs, whose OnCollisionEnter2D tests
//                     collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Player") and loads the
//                     Overworld. It is a SECOND solid collider in the cave and
//                     it is the object a tag-based ground check gets wrong.
//                     This is the lesson's tag-versus-layer teaching moment and
//                     it is sitting in the student's own Hierarchy.
//     (`Quad` is the backdrop: MeshCollider + MeshRenderer, layer Default,
//      scale 48. A MeshCollider is 3D, so Physics2D cannot see it — which is
//      why the "mask = Everything" bug hunt lists three hit-able objects, not
//      four.)
//
//   Assets/Prefabs/Player.prefab — root: Transform + PlayerController ONLY.
//     Tag `Player` (SceneSwitch depends on it), layer 0. NO Rigidbody2D and NO
//     Collider2D: both are the student's own work from 2.1 (CircleCollider2D on
//     the root + Rigidbody2D Dynamic). The only child is `Sprite` at local
//     position (0, 0.6, -1), localScale 2 — so THE ROOT IS NOT THE CENTRE OF
//     THE CHARACTER, which the old text asserted. Corrected: the art hangs
//     above the root, and the origin is a measurement the student makes, not an
//     assumption. (The Overworld's Player instance in the Platformer scene is a
//     prefab instance with m_AddedComponents: [] and m_RemovedComponents: [], so
//     the scene carries no body either — the stale `isPlatformer: 1` override on
//     the PlayerController is a leftover from the stripped .cs and is not relied
//     on anywhere in this lesson.)
//
//   ProjectSettings/Physics2DSettings.asset
//     m_QueriesStartInColliders: 1  — raycasts that START INSIDE a collider DO
//       report that collider in this project. So the self-hit bug ("a ray fired
//       from the root reports the Player at distance zero, forever") is REAL
//       and reproducible here, not hypothetical.
//     m_QueriesHitTriggers: 1 — triggers are hit by queries too.
//   (Also verified: Player_Shovel.controller carries exactly IsWalking/Bool,
//   WalkDir/Int, Attack/Trigger, so the Tinker item about a fourth parameter is
//   accurate; Player_Axe.controller is the equivalent asset 5.1 swaps in;
//   Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts/TopDown_EnemyAnimator.cs exists and reads
//   Direction + Attack with no ground check of any kind.)
//
// NOT REINTRODUCED: the old claim that a Physics tilemap's Rigidbody2D has
// "Gravity Scale 0". The Inspector says 1 and the field is greyed out, because
// Body Type is Static and gravity only ever acts on a Dynamic body. That is the
// correction made in an earlier session and it is kept, as a teaching moment,
// in step 1 of the walkthrough.
//
// SCAFFOLDING: GUIDED (level 2), and asks.ts agrees. 6.2 reuses the 6.1 jump —
// a line the student has already written — and adds exactly one idea with no
// precedent anywhere in the course: asking the physics engine a question about
// the world and writing the answer down. Everything else in the file TELLS the
// engine what to do; a raycast ASKS. So the shape of the whole file is given
// (both the script and the Inspector work, because the layer is project
// settings and the origin is an empty child) and exactly one line is the
// student's: the raycast assignment. It carries a `what` prompt and a `hint`
// that names what to reach for without printing `Physics2D.Raycast`.
// The decompose block withholds exactly one part for the same reason, and its
// `doc` pointer stays visible — finding the answer in the reading is the skill.
//
// The walkthrough builds the RAYCAST row of the four-technique table, because
// 7.2 already promises the student wrote "a LayerMask field and passed it into
// Physics2D.Raycast". The other three rows stay in the table: choosing between
// them is the design beat, and the Build Lab sends a student who chose a
// different row down their own road.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — ids are POSITIONAL (src/lib/formify.ts), so nothing here
// is reordered, renumbered or re-counted. All four keep their document
// position, their exact `<li>` counts, and — the part that is easy to miss —
// their exact SET OF BOXES: formify only gives a box to an item whose text
// contains a '?' or does not end in '.' or ':', so an item reworded to *gain* a
// question mark would renumber every box below it in the same block. The
// baseline inventory, unchanged:
//   socratic  "Asking Versus Remembering"  5 <li>, 5 boxes  (6.2:sc1:q1..q5)
//   socratic  "Choose Your Check"          6 <li>, 5 boxes  (6.2:sc2:q1..q5;
//                                                    item 2 is deliberately
