const WALK = {
  ask: 'why can I jump forever in mid-air?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  intro:
    'Three Inspector fields, one empty child object, one method that asks a question every frame, and one term added to the jump condition you already have. The layer work is not code at all, and the raycast itself is a single line — that line is the one thing here with nothing earlier in the course to copy.',
  level: 2,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Name the ground before you can ask about it — this step is not code',
      lead: 'A mask is a filter, and a filter needs something to filter on. This project has the tag `Ground` and no `Ground` layer, so the first job is to make the bucket and put the right things in it.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// Edit ▸ Project Settings ▸ Tags and Layers  ▸  an empty User Layer  =  Ground', what: 'Scroll the **Layers** list: `Default`, `TransparentFX`, `Ignore Raycast`, `Water`, `UI`, and a run of empty slots under them. Type `Ground` into the first empty one and close the window. Note what did **not** happen — no object in your project changed. A layer is a name for an empty bucket until something is put in it, and layers are project-wide, not per-scene, so you do this once for the whole game.' , why: '**A tag and a layer are not the same tool, and this project has been using the tag.** The `Collision` tilemap is already tagged `Ground`, which is what a `CompareTag` check reads. A raycast mask cannot use a tag — it needs a **layer** — and there is no `Ground` layer in this project. Skip this step and the mask field you declare in step 3 has only `Default` and `Everything` to offer, and both of those include your own body.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '// Platformer ▸ Grid ▸ Collision      ▸ Layer = Ground', what: 'The cave floor and walls, and the thing your character has been landing on since 6.1. Read its Inspector while you are there: a `TilemapCollider2D`, a `CompositeCollider2D`, and a `Rigidbody2D` whose **Body Type is Static**. Collision already works — this lesson is not about making the floor solid, it is about asking the floor a question.' , why: '**Read that body\'s Gravity Scale before you judge it.** It says `1`, and it is greyed out, and both of those are correct. Gravity Scale is a multiplier on the gravity the physics engine applies to a body — and a **Static** body is never moved by anything, so there is nothing for the number to act on and Unity disables the field. It is the same rule you met on the coin in 2.1 and again on the enemy in 4.1, seen from the other side: the body type decides who is allowed to move something, and gravity only ever applies to the one type that is. Do not "fix" the `1` — switch this tilemap to Dynamic and the floor itself starts falling.' },
        { code: '// Platformer ▸ Grid ▸ Cave Exit      ▸ Layer = Ground', what: '**The second solid object in this cave**, and the one nobody remembers. It carries the same three components as the floor and the same Static body — and it is **Untagged**, because it was built for a different job: `SceneSwitch.cs` sits on it and sends you back to the Overworld when you touch it.' , why: '**This is the object a ground check written against a tag gets wrong, and it is why this lesson is about layers.** Two solid surfaces, one tag between them. A tag answers "is this that one particular thing?", and a layer answers "is this the kind of thing I care about?". Put the floor in the bucket and leave the cave mouth out and your check is honest on everything you painted and guessing on everything else — silently, because a ray does exactly what its mask tells it to.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '// Platformer ▸ Grid ▸ No Collision  ▸ Layer stays Default', what: 'Leave it alone. This tilemap is paint: a `TilemapRenderer` and **no collider of any kind**. A raycast reports colliders, and there is nothing here for one to report.' },
