      title: 'Declare the point, the reach, the filter and the answer',
      lead: 'Four lines in the class body, beside `moveSpeed` and the jump field from 6.1. Three are `[SerializeField]` knobs a designer can see in the Inspector — the pattern from 1.3 — and the fourth is the answer itself.',
      lines: [
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private Transform groundCheck;', what: 'The slot that will hold the empty child from step 2. A `Transform` is a position in the world, and wired from the Inspector it is also a reference to another object — the same kind of slot as the waypoints in 4.1.' , why: '**Wire it at the end of this step, before you press Play.** An empty slot does not complain when the scene loads; it throws a `NullReferenceException` at the first line that **reads** it, and this script reads it sixty times a second, so the Console fills faster than you can read it.' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private float groundCheckDistance = 0.1f;', what: 'How far down the ray reaches. `0.1` is a starting guess. The honest value is **measured**: the gap from the bottom of your collider to the tile surface, plus a hair.' , why: '**This number is the difference between a check and a lie, and you cannot read it off the screen.** Too short and the ray misses the floor by a hair while your feet are visibly on it, so the jump stops working at random. Too long and the character is "grounded" while standing over thin air beside a ledge — the exact bug the Bug Hunt makes you produce on purpose.' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private LayerMask groundLayer;', what: '**What the ray is allowed to notice.** The type is `LayerMask`, not `int`, and the Inspector draws it as a dropdown of layer names rather than a number.' , why: '**A `LayerMask` is an integer whose bits mark which layers you care about** — and that is the whole trick. It is why "Everything" is not a special case in the code: it is simply every bit switched on. Leave the field empty and the mask is `Nothing`, and a ray allowed to hit nothing reports nothing; point it at `Everything` and the ray is allowed to hit your own body.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
