  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

// PlayerController.cs at the end of 6.2 — everything from 1.4 through 6.1,
// plus the one question this script has never asked: am I standing on
// something? Every other line here TELLS the engine what to do. CheckGround()
// ASKS it something, and CheckJump() is the only line that acts on the answer.
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;
    [SerializeField] private float jumpForce = 7f;          // yours, from 6.1

    // Today's three knobs, all visible in the Inspector (1.3).
    [SerializeField] private Transform groundCheck;              // where the ray starts
    [SerializeField] private float groundCheckDistance = 0.1f;   // how far down it reaches
    [SerializeField] private LayerMask groundLayer;              // what it may notice

    private Rigidbody2D body;
    private Animator animator;
    private SpriteRenderer spriteRenderer;

    // The answer. In the class body, because the check writes it and the jump
    // reads it — and re-derived every frame, because nothing ever tells you it
    // changed.
    private bool isGrounded;

    void Start()
    {
        // Looked up once, never inside Update() — the rule from 1.6 and 2.2.
        body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
        animator = GetComponentInChildren<Animator>();
        spriteRenderer = GetComponentInChildren<SpriteRenderer>();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // ... your input, movement, gravity and animation lines from
        // 1.4, 1.6, 2.2 and 6.1, unchanged and left out here ...

        CheckGround();   // ask
        CheckJump();     // act on the answer
        UpdateAnimation();
    }

    // The whole lesson. A ray from the point under your feet, one direction,
    // one distance, one mask — and the result assigned straight into the bool,
    // because a RaycastHit2D converts to true when it hit and false when it
    // did not. Nothing here moves anything: a question is not an action.
    void CheckGround()
    {
        isGrounded = Physics2D.Raycast(groundCheck.position, Vector2.down, groundCheckDistance, groundLayer);
    }

    // The jump from 6.1 with one term in front of it. Leave the term out and
    // the character can climb the sky.
    void CheckJump()
    {
        if (isGrounded && Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space))
        {
            body.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
        }
    }

    // The same three inputs the ray uses, drawn. Keep the Player selected in
    // the Hierarchy while you play, and watch this go red the frame you leave
    // the ground.
    private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
    {
        if (groundCheck == null)
        {
            return;
        }

        Gizmos.color = isGrounded ? Color.green : Color.red;
        Gizmos.DrawLine(groundCheck.position, groundCheck.position + Vector3.down * groundCheckDistance);
    }

    // ... UpdateAnimation(), CheckAttack(), the pickups and the damage, unchanged ...
}

// Edit ▸ Project Settings ▸ Tags and Layers:
//   the first empty User Layer  =  Ground
//
// Inspector, on Assets/Prefabs/Player ▸ PlayerController:
//   Ground Check            =  GroundCheck   <- an empty child sitting on the
//                                               BOTTOM EDGE of your collider
//   Ground Check Distance   =  0.1           <- measured, not guessed
//   Ground Layer            =  Ground        <- Ground, and nothing else
//
// The layer goes on BOTH solid tilemaps under the cave's Grid — Collision and
// Cave Exit. The third one, No Collision, has no collider at all, so no ray can
// ever hit it whatever layer it is on.`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. The gap is the raycast assignment and it is the one line here with nothing earlier in the course to copy: a point, a direction, a distance and a mask, in that order. This version assigns the result straight into the `bool`, because a `RaycastHit2D` converts to true or false on its own — writing `.collider != null` on the end is equally correct and slightly more explicit, and either one passes. What has to match is the shape, not the text: the answer is computed in exactly one place, it is computed every frame rather than stored when you land, and the jump asks for it before it fires. Your field names, your key, your force and your movement method from 2.2 are all yours.',
};

const body = `<h1>6.2 — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your jump works. That is the problem. Tap the key in mid-air and you jump again, and again,
    and again — a jump with no ground check is not a jump, it is a jetpack with infinite fuel.
    Today you teach the script to ask a question it has never asked:
    <em>am I standing on something?</em>
</p>

${renderAskBanner('why can I jump forever in mid-air?', 2)}

${renderDecompose('6.2', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: You Can Jump in Mid-Air</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Open <code>Assets/_Scenes/Platformer.unity</code> — the cave — and press Play. Jump once,
        then keep tapping the key while you are in the air. You climb. Nothing stops you, and the
        Console says nothing, because from the computer's point of view your code is working
        exactly as written: <em>if the key went down, add an impulse</em>. There is no rule about
        where you are allowed to do that.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Your goal: a jump that only fires when the character is standing on
        something.</strong> So start with the honest question — how would a script ever
        <em>know</em> it is standing on something? Nothing on the Rigidbody2D means "grounded".
        There is no event that fires when you touch a floor and another when you leave it. You
        have to go and ask.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The floor is already there. The question is not.</strong> Open the cave, expand
        <code>Grid</code> in the Hierarchy and select the <code>Collision</code> tilemap. It
        already carries a <strong>TilemapCollider2D</strong>, a <strong>CompositeCollider2D</strong>
        and a <strong>Rigidbody2D</strong> — and that body's <strong>Body Type is Static</strong>,
        which is why your character lands on it instead of falling through. Collision already works.
        What does not exist yet is any code that <em>asks about</em> it, and that is the entire
        lesson.
        <br><br>
        <strong>Read that body's Gravity Scale before you touch it.</strong> It says <code>1</code>,
        and it is greyed out — and both of those are correct. Gravity Scale only means anything to a
        <em>Dynamic</em> body, because only a Dynamic body is moved by the physics engine. A Static
        body is never moved by anything, so Unity has nothing for that setting to do and disables
        the field. This is the same rule you met on the coin in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> and again
        on the enemy in <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>, seen from the other side: the body type decides
        who is allowed to move something, and the gravity scale only ever applies to the one type
        that is allowed. Do not "fix" the <code>1</code> — the moment you switch this tilemap to
        Dynamic, the ground starts falling.
    </div>

    <p>
        Now count the surfaces. Under <code>Grid</code> there are three tilemaps —
        <code>No Collision</code>, <code>Collision</code> and <code>Cave Exit</code> — and only
        <strong>one</strong> of them carries the tag <code>Ground</code>. That single fact decides
        half of this lesson, so look at it properly before you read on.
    </p>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>There is a second solid object in this cave, and it is untagged.</strong> Select
        <code>Cave Exit</code> and read its Inspector: the same three components as the floor — a
        TilemapCollider2D, a CompositeCollider2D and a Static Rigidbody2D — and
        <strong>Untagged</strong>, because it was built to do a different job. <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code>
        sits on it, and when your collider touches it you are sent back to the Overworld.
        <br><br>
        So you have two solid surfaces and one tag between them. That is the difference between a
        <em>tag</em> and a <em>layer</em>, sitting in your own Hierarchy, and it is not a technicality:
        a tag answers <em>"is this that one particular thing?"</em> and a layer answers <em>"is this
        the kind of thing I care about?"</em>. Only the second question still gives the right answer
        when somebody adds a third floor tilemap next week. You can compare a tag to a string; you
        can hand a layer to the physics engine and let <em>it</em> do the filtering.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Asking Versus Remembering</h3>
        <ol>
<li>A tag and a layer are both just names attached to an object. Why can a raycast be told to ignore everything except one layer, but not "everything except one tag"?</li>
<li>The <code>Collision</code> tilemap is tagged <code>Ground</code> and the <code>Cave Exit</code> tilemap beside it is not, and both of them are solid. If you wrote a ground check that compared tags, would it answer yes everywhere you can stand — and what would it cost you every frame you ran it?</li>
<li>Your character is standing still on the floor. Is "grounded" a <em>fact you could store</em> when you land, or a <em>fact you should re-check</em> every frame? What could change it without any code of yours running?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: Four Ways to Ask About the Ground</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every platformer ever shipped uses one of these. Read all four — the differences are in
        what each one can <em>tell you</em> and where each one lies to you. Then pick one, because
        you are going to live with the choice for the rest of the unit.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>The question you are asking</th><th>What it needs</th><th>What it returns</th><th>Where it lies to you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Physics2D.Raycast(origin, Vector2.down, distance, mask)</code></td>
            <td>"Is there a ground-layer collider directly below this exact point?"</td>
            <td>An origin (usually your feet, not your centre), a distance, and a <strong>LayerMask</strong>.</td>
            <td>A <code>RaycastHit2D</code>: the collider, the distance to it, the exact contact point and the surface normal.</td>
            <td>It is one infinitely thin line. Stand with one pixel of your feet over the edge of a tile and it reports <em>air</em>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Physics2D.OverlapCircle(point, radius, mask)</code></td>
            <td>"Is there a ground-layer collider anywhere inside this circle at my feet?"</td>
            <td>A point, a radius, and a LayerMask.</td>
            <td>The collider only — no direction, no distance, no contact point.</td>
            <td>A wall beside your feet is inside the circle too, so you can be "grounded" against a vertical surface.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collider2D.IsTouchingLayers(mask)</code></td>
            <td>"Is my own collider touching anything on this layer, right now?"</td>
            <td>Your Collider2D and a LayerMask. No origin, no distance, no ray.</td>
            <td>A single true or false.</td>
            <td>It can only see contacts that physically exist — a <strong>Kinematic</strong> body needs <em>Use Full Kinematic Contacts</em> ticked or it will report false while standing on the floor.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnCollisionStay2D(Collision2D c)</code> with a layer or tag check</td>
            <td>"What am I in contact with during this physics step?"</td>
            <td>A callback method, plus a rule to stop it re-firing your jump every step.</td>
            <td>The whole collision: the object, the contact points, the relative velocity.</td>
            <td>It fires continuously, so a naive jump fires continuously too; and it does not fire at all for a Kinematic body against static colliders unless full kinematic contacts are enabled. Even with them enabled you get the <em>message</em>, not a collision — the Kinematic body still passes through the static collider, which is why the full kinematic contacts checkbox is a way to <em>hear</em> a floor, never a way to stand on one.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Two names from the docs that are the same idea.</strong> A <code>LayerMask</code>
        field in the Inspector looks like a dropdown, but it is an integer whose bits mark which
        layers you care about. That is why "Everything" is not a special case in the code — it is
        simply all the bits switched on, which is exactly why it is the setting that produces the
        classic bug in a moment. And a <code>tag</code> is not a layer: the cave's floor already has
        the tag and the project has no matching layer at all, so the first thing you build today is
        not code.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choose Your Check</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Which of the four did you pick, and which one does your character's body argue for? A character with a wide flat collider and one with a small round one do not fail the same way under the same technique.</li>
<li>Two of the four can tell you how far the ground is. Name a game behaviour that needs that distance, and say whether your game has it.</li>
<li>The circle check would call a wall "ground". Is that a bug you would fix, or a feature a wall-slide or wall-jump would later exploit?</li>
<li>The collision-callback check needs no mask and no distance — it is the laziest to write. What does it demand instead, and which Rigidbody2D checkbox can silently break it?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Prove It, Then Break It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — the honesty test.</strong> Press Play with the Player selected in the
        Hierarchy so the gizmo draws, and watch it through four deliberate actions: stand still
        (<em>true</em>), jump (<em>false</em> the moment you leave the ground), land
        (<em>true</em>), walk off a ledge (<em>false</em>). If any of those four is wrong, your
        <strong>distance</strong> or your <strong>origin</strong> is wrong — not your logic. Fix
        it by moving the empty child or changing the number, and run the four again.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — walk the whole cave with the gizmo on.</strong> Find every place the line
        is red while your feet are visibly on something solid. Each one is a surface that is missing
        from your <code>Ground</code> layer — and there is at least one, because this cave has two
        solid tilemaps and only one of them was tagged. Decide which of the three tilemaps under
        <code>Grid</code> belong in the bucket and which one cannot possibly matter, and write the
        reason down before you tick anything.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — the mask experiment.</strong> Set your mask to <strong>Everything</strong>
        and press Play. Stand still on the floor, then jump. If your origin sits inside your own
        collider, the ray hits <em>you</em> at distance zero and reports ground forever — the
        jetpack is back, and now it is wearing a disguise. Put the mask back to <code>Ground</code>
        and confirm the check is honest again. Then set it to <strong>Nothing</strong> and watch the
        opposite failure: a completely silent character who cannot jump at all.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — tune the distance by measurement.</strong> Put the character on the floor,
        read the real gap from the bottom of your collider to the tile surface in the Scene view,
        and set the number from that. Then halve it, play, and watch the jump start failing on
        ground you can see. Guessing here is what produces the whole of the Bug Hunt below.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the check.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which technique did you choose, and what are its exact inputs — origin, direction, distance, mask? If you chose anything other than the raycast the walkthrough built, describe how those four inputs map onto yours.</li>
            <li>Where does the origin sit, and how did you <em>find out</em> rather than assume? The Player root is not the centre of the character you can see, and it is not automatically your feet.</li>
            <li>How far is "down"? Measure the real gap from your collider's bottom edge to the tile surface, then write that number down. Guessing here is what produces a jump that fails at random.</li>
            <li>Where does the mask come from — a serialized <code>LayerMask</code> field, or a hard-coded layer name? Which one lets a teammate fix your level without opening your script?</li>
            <li>Where is the result stored, and what type is it? A <code>bool</code> named for the <em>question</em> ("am I grounded") reads better than one named for the technique.</li>
            <li>Does the check run in <code>Update()</code> or <code>FixedUpdate()</code>? Remember that the answer is a physics answer, produced by physics, on the physics clock.</li>
            <li>Is the jump condition now compound? Write the whole sentence in English before you write it in C#.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: making the invisible visible.</strong> Before you trust
        a check you cannot see, draw it. <code>OnDrawGizmosSelected</code> runs in the editor and can
        draw a line from your origin along your check direction, scaled by your distance. Three lines
        of code turn "my jump doesn't work" into "my whisker is too short" in about four seconds.
        Draw it in one colour when the check is false and another when it is true — and notice that
        drawing it costs your game nothing, because nothing in a gizmo method ships.
    </div>
</div>


<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain the difference between a tag and a layer, using two solid tilemaps from your own cave as the example</li>
        <li>Create a layer, assign it to every surface you can land on, and say which object must <em>not</em> get it — and why it cannot matter</li>
        <li>Compare the four ground-check techniques and defend your choice</li>
        <li>Show the check with a gizmo and tune the distance by measurement, not by guessing</li>
        <li>Explain the self-hit bug, the Nothing mask, and why a LayerMask is what stands between you and both of them</li>
        <li>Explain why the grounded value is re-derived every frame instead of stored when you land</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

